1
00:00:06,206 --> 00:00:09,209
[car engine starts, revving]

2
00:00:10,076 --> 00:00:11,144
[tires screeching]

3
00:00:11,311 --> 00:00:12,979
[upbeat instrumental music plays]

4
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:17,033
[Jerry] All right, let’s get real.

5
00:00:17,099 --> 00:00:21,133
This is a 1964 Volvo 122 sedan,

6
00:00:21,233 --> 00:00:24,433
in pearl white
with a red leatherette interior,

7
00:00:24,599 --> 00:00:28,000
and red wheels with hubcaps
that have red insignias.

8
00:00:29,733 --> 00:00:31,866
In Europe, these cars were known 
as Amazons.

9
00:00:32,032 --> 00:00:33,833
[car engine revs]

10
00:00:35,900 --> 00:00:38,833
It’s got a four cylinder engine 
and a four-speed gear box.

11
00:00:39,133 --> 00:00:40,733
It has 90 horsepower.

12
00:00:41,566 --> 00:00:42,633
That’s enough.

13
00:00:43,500 --> 00:00:46,733
This was also the very first 
production car to come with seat belts

14
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:48,900
and a padded safety dashboard.

15
00:00:51,266 --> 00:00:54,366
Cars are always the most vivid expression 
of what a culture is about

16
00:00:54,466 --> 00:00:56,000
in the time that it was made.

17
00:00:56,166 --> 00:00:59,300
This car is the essence
of great '60s Swedish values.

18
00:00:59,466 --> 00:01:03,099
Honest, hardworking, unfussy, 
not out to impress,

19
00:01:03,366 --> 00:01:04,633
and built to last.

20
00:01:06,633 --> 00:01:10,733
It also happens to be the personal taste 
of my unbelievably talented

21
00:01:10,833 --> 00:01:12,899
and funny extra special guest today,

22
00:01:13,066 --> 00:01:14,800
Ms. Kristen Wiig.

23
00:01:14,966 --> 00:01:18,100
Kristen is of Norwegian descent and loves 
old Volvos.

24
00:01:18,200 --> 00:01:19,600
I think there’s a connection there.

25
00:01:20,966 --> 00:01:23,100
I know Sweden and Norway are 
two different countries,

26
00:01:23,233 --> 00:01:24,633
but come on, are they really?

27
00:01:25,666 --> 00:01:29,100
It’s also a perfect car for the kind of
hipster neighborhood that she lives in. 

28
00:01:30,233 --> 00:01:32,733
Hipsters seem to like things
that are old and out of it

29
00:01:32,899 --> 00:01:35,399
to show how young and into it they are.

30
00:01:35,766 --> 00:01:37,800
Like bicycles with only one gear.

31
00:01:38,966 --> 00:01:40,266
How dumb.

32
00:01:40,766 --> 00:01:42,899
But the bottom of my jeans are rolled up,

33
00:01:43,166 --> 00:01:45,433
my sunglasses have translucent frames,

34
00:01:45,766 --> 00:01:47,533
I’m ready to have a hipster day.

35
00:01:48,408 --> 00:01:49,876
[telephone ringing]

36
00:01:50,133 --> 00:01:50,966
[Kristen] Hello?

37
00:01:51,033 --> 00:01:52,633
Kristen, it’s Jerry Seinfeld. 

38
00:01:52,833 --> 00:01:54,333
[Kristen] Oh, my gosh, hi.

39
00:01:54,433 --> 00:01:55,933
[Jerry] What kind of morning have you had?

40
00:01:56,000 --> 00:01:57,133
[Kristen] A great morning.

41
00:01:57,250 --> 00:02:00,487
I actually drink this stuff called
mushroom coffee.

42
00:02:00,553 --> 00:02:03,890
[Jerry] Well, I know a place that makes
moss and wood bark omelets.

43
00:02:04,290 --> 00:02:05,625
[Kristen chuckles]

44
00:02:06,359 --> 00:02:11,931
I’m Jerry Seinfeld, and this is
Comedians in Cars Getting Coffee.

45
00:02:16,633 --> 00:02:17,799
Oh, my gosh.

46
00:02:17,933 --> 00:02:19,600
[jazz music playing]

47
00:02:19,772 --> 00:02:21,741
-Are you kidding?
-You know what this is?

48
00:02:21,808 --> 00:02:24,177
[Kristen] It’s an Amazon. [laughs]

49
00:02:24,299 --> 00:02:27,966
-Oh, my God, Jerry, this is insane.
-You’re going to lose your mind.

50
00:02:28,166 --> 00:02:29,799
-I already have.
-You’re going, yeah.

51
00:02:29,966 --> 00:02:31,333
[Jerry] When you open the door,

52
00:02:31,433 --> 00:02:32,666
tell me how that feels.

53
00:02:33,600 --> 00:02:36,033
Oh, yeah. It’s a solid click.

54
00:02:36,133 --> 00:02:37,833
[Kristen exhales]

55
00:02:38,925 --> 00:02:40,126
[giggles]

56
00:02:40,200 --> 00:02:41,333
Oh, thank you.

57
00:02:41,633 --> 00:02:44,666
-A stranger just handed me my...
-Oh, good, are you doing a western later?

58
00:02:44,733 --> 00:02:46,000
[both laughing]

59
00:02:46,200 --> 00:02:48,400
[jazz music playing]

60
00:02:51,704 --> 00:02:54,040
-[Kristen] Oh, my gosh, it’s a tank.
-[Jerry] It's a tank.

61
00:02:57,133 --> 00:02:59,100
[Kristen]
My boyfriend is very into Volvos.

62
00:02:59,266 --> 00:03:01,933
He is so excited about the Volvos.

63
00:03:02,533 --> 00:03:04,333
-And actually we, um...
-How did that happen?

64
00:03:04,400 --> 00:03:05,333
How did that come up?

65
00:03:05,566 --> 00:03:07,733
Well, his parents have,
I think they have three,

66
00:03:07,866 --> 00:03:09,666
and he drove one in high school,

67
00:03:09,866 --> 00:03:12,466
had kind of mentioned that
he drove Volvos, like old Volvos

68
00:03:12,533 --> 00:03:14,666
and I was like, "I love old Volvos."

69
00:03:14,933 --> 00:03:18,866
How long did you know your boyfriend
before this information came out?

70
00:03:18,965 --> 00:03:22,202
-Were you sleeping together?
-He did love that I liked them so much.

71
00:03:22,302 --> 00:03:24,037
I mean, it definitely was a bonding thing.

72
00:03:24,333 --> 00:03:26,466
[Jerry] I love two people
getting heated up

73
00:03:26,533 --> 00:03:28,666
and jumping into bed
because of an old Volvo.

74
00:03:28,766 --> 00:03:31,033
-That is hilarious.
-I know, right? It’s kind of rare.

75
00:03:31,100 --> 00:03:33,100
[slow jazz playing]

76
00:03:35,466 --> 00:03:37,133
Isn’t this nice and relaxing?

77
00:03:39,233 --> 00:03:40,133
[Kristen] Yeah.

78
00:03:41,500 --> 00:03:42,433
So relaxing.

79
00:03:44,633 --> 00:03:46,666
-Come on-- Yeah, the breeze.
-The breeze is nice.

80
00:03:48,033 --> 00:03:49,500
The Volvo-ness.

81
00:03:53,132 --> 00:03:56,102
Oh, my God, I saw the camera over there,
I was like, "They’re filming us."

82
00:03:56,869 --> 00:03:58,771
-I didn’t quite--
-I’ve had that same reaction.

83
00:03:58,838 --> 00:04:00,607
-"Leave us alone!"
-I thought, "Oh, my..."

84
00:04:00,673 --> 00:04:01,808
"We’re just people."

85
00:04:02,033 --> 00:04:04,166
[jazz music playing]

86
00:04:05,878 --> 00:04:07,580
[inaudible]

87
00:04:11,600 --> 00:04:14,333
-[Jerry] What do you do for fun?
-[Kristen] I play the ukulele.

88
00:04:14,466 --> 00:04:18,133
And I’m working on
a ukulele album right now.

89
00:04:18,433 --> 00:04:20,099
Waterskiing, things like that.

90
00:04:20,500 --> 00:04:23,800
You waterskiing is a really funny image.

91
00:04:23,866 --> 00:04:26,333
I just slalomed for the first time.
It’s a good workout.

92
00:04:26,400 --> 00:04:27,666
Oh, it’s idiotic.

93
00:04:29,099 --> 00:04:31,099
[upbeat jazz music playing]

94
00:04:31,599 --> 00:04:32,933
[inaudible]

95
00:04:33,933 --> 00:04:36,900
-Oh, this is a great bookstore.
-Let’s go in for the smell.

96
00:04:37,033 --> 00:04:38,200
[sniffing]

97
00:04:38,266 --> 00:04:39,466
Oh, yeah.

98
00:04:41,300 --> 00:04:42,900
-[Jerry] So you like LA?
-[Kristen] I do.

99
00:04:43,000 --> 00:04:45,200
-[Jerry] How come?
-I think when I moved here,

100
00:04:45,300 --> 00:04:48,900
when I was in the Groundlings,
it was just such a great time for me.

101
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:52,066
I originally came out here ‘cause
I wanted to act and stuff.

102
00:04:52,233 --> 00:04:55,566
But I got here and I was like,
"What do I do? I have no experience."

103
00:04:55,633 --> 00:04:57,033
-So I didn’t do anything.
-Right.

104
00:04:57,096 --> 00:05:00,466
I went and saw the show
and I’d never seen improv in my life.

105
00:05:00,533 --> 00:05:02,035
-I didn’t know what it was.
-Right.

106
00:05:02,101 --> 00:05:04,137
So getting suggestions,
I'm like, "What’s going on?

107
00:05:04,203 --> 00:05:06,105
What are they gonna say?"
It was so funny and I was like,

108
00:05:06,172 --> 00:05:07,407
"I want to do that."

109
00:05:07,473 --> 00:05:09,809
-Right.
-Like I feel like my brain is better

110
00:05:09,900 --> 00:05:11,133
at something like that,

111
00:05:11,200 --> 00:05:13,833
than reading a script and then doing it

112
00:05:13,880 --> 00:05:15,815
and then people can be like,
"Is that good?"

113
00:05:16,066 --> 00:05:18,966
-I still do shows there once in a while.
-You’re kidding. Oh, my God.

114
00:05:19,018 --> 00:05:22,355
And sometimes you’re on the floor,
you’re sitting there, just like my home.

115
00:05:24,023 --> 00:05:25,425
[inaudible]

116
00:05:26,292 --> 00:05:29,028
-Oh, look at that. That looks great.
-Oh, my God. That looks amazing,

117
00:05:29,128 --> 00:05:30,530
-those fries?
-Oh, good for you.

118
00:05:30,596 --> 00:05:33,199
Look at that, he ordered--
he ordered a dog.

119
00:05:34,400 --> 00:05:35,466
Don’t eat it.

120
00:05:35,568 --> 00:05:37,670
[dramatic instrumental music playing]

121
00:05:43,433 --> 00:05:45,966
You know what? I’ve never been
inside House of Pies.

122
00:05:46,033 --> 00:05:47,666
We’re going to House of Pies.

123
00:05:48,099 --> 00:05:49,400
Oh, this is amazing.

124
00:05:49,633 --> 00:05:52,266
[dramatic music continues]

125
00:06:01,861 --> 00:06:03,129
[Kristen gasps]

126
00:06:03,333 --> 00:06:04,700
Smells like crust.

127
00:06:04,866 --> 00:06:06,966
[jazz music playing]

128
00:06:11,099 --> 00:06:13,833
The greatest bit I’ve ever seen
on an awards show,

129
00:06:13,900 --> 00:06:15,733
is when you and Will
did the Golden Globes,

130
00:06:15,900 --> 00:06:18,233
‘cause everyone was laughing before
you started.

131
00:06:18,366 --> 00:06:22,133
You walked out there with this look 
of wonder, at the Golden Globes.

132
00:06:22,266 --> 00:06:24,133
The whoriest night of the year 
in Hollywood.

133
00:06:24,666 --> 00:06:26,033
A town of whores.

134
00:06:26,200 --> 00:06:27,966
[audience laughing]

135
00:06:29,666 --> 00:06:32,099
We hadn’t seen the movies.

136
00:06:32,400 --> 00:06:34,966
-Mariole, Mariole Streep.
-Mariole Streep. Mariole Streep.

137
00:06:37,033 --> 00:06:40,866
-Hope Springs and she’s the sassy sheriff.
-Hope Springs, my gosh and she plays...

138
00:06:41,000 --> 00:06:43,666
The only thing we knew we were going
to say is that "get out" part.

139
00:06:43,799 --> 00:06:47,366
-She says, "You, you get out of here."
-"You, you, you get out of here."

140
00:06:47,440 --> 00:06:49,542
-[audience laughing]
-[chuckling]

141
00:06:52,766 --> 00:06:54,066
There’s the guy who ate the dog.

142
00:06:54,200 --> 00:06:56,766
-He didn’t eat the dog.
-Well, we can’t see his legs.

143
00:06:57,066 --> 00:06:59,433
That dog does look edible, by the way.

144
00:06:59,866 --> 00:07:02,000
-He’s like already charbroiled.
-Yeah.

145
00:07:02,088 --> 00:07:03,055
[both laughing]

146
00:07:03,122 --> 00:07:05,658
[jazz music playing]

147
00:07:06,025 --> 00:07:07,193
[bell dings]

148
00:07:07,799 --> 00:07:10,766
I mean, I didn’t get SNL until I was 30.

149
00:07:10,900 --> 00:07:12,000
-Really?
-Yeah.

150
00:07:12,099 --> 00:07:14,000
I actually waited tables
at Universal Studios,

151
00:07:14,099 --> 00:07:15,599
in the executive dining room.

152
00:07:15,666 --> 00:07:19,466
So I see people now and I’m like,
"I used to give you Arnold Palmers."

153
00:07:20,400 --> 00:07:22,533
I worked in a flower shop
for like three years.

154
00:07:22,700 --> 00:07:25,466
It’s like a floral design studio,
is what they call it.

155
00:07:25,812 --> 00:07:27,547
[smacks lips, laughs]

156
00:07:27,666 --> 00:07:28,666
That’s a character.

157
00:07:28,799 --> 00:07:32,166
Someone that works as a florist,
and is constantly making the "pop" sound.

158
00:07:32,599 --> 00:07:35,000
Oh, these are the times I wish
I was still on the show.

159
00:07:35,233 --> 00:07:36,599
That’s what you’re great at.

160
00:07:36,766 --> 00:07:41,166
You see somebody twirling their hair and
you just fill in the rest of the picture.

161
00:07:41,266 --> 00:07:44,200
I take like little things people like
say or do, and you’re like,

162
00:07:44,299 --> 00:07:46,599
"What if I just took that,
and then like, exploded it."

163
00:07:46,700 --> 00:07:50,599
Like, I did this character on SNL,
Aunt Linda,

164
00:07:50,636 --> 00:07:53,639
I don’t know if you ever saw it.
She’s a movie critic. It started because

165
00:07:53,706 --> 00:07:56,442
I was on a plane,
and they were showingThe Matrix

166
00:07:56,509 --> 00:07:59,779
and this woman was so confused by
the movie.

167
00:07:59,846 --> 00:08:03,382
And so loud about it because she had
her headphones on, and she’s like,

168
00:08:03,449 --> 00:08:06,452
"What? Now where are they?"

169
00:08:06,519 --> 00:08:09,422
And she was doing it so loud
and I just took my head--

170
00:08:09,489 --> 00:08:11,791
and I was writing down things
she was saying.

171
00:08:11,891 --> 00:08:14,760
-She’s like, "Now they’re flying? What?"
-That is so...

172
00:08:18,431 --> 00:08:20,766
What was the first thing you did
that you felt,

173
00:08:20,866 --> 00:08:22,033
"I think this works?"

174
00:08:22,133 --> 00:08:25,233
I like doing the Target lady.
I think I did it in my audition.

175
00:08:25,400 --> 00:08:29,866
We have one candle
that’s got like a pine scent on the top,

176
00:08:30,033 --> 00:08:32,366
and on the bottom it’s like a nutmeg.

177
00:08:32,833 --> 00:08:36,566
So the top when you burn that down,
it’s a whole other candle.

178
00:08:36,933 --> 00:08:40,133
You know, that show it’s like you
don’t hear for a while, it’s very like,

179
00:08:40,266 --> 00:08:41,199
ambiguous.

180
00:08:41,299 --> 00:08:43,100
I did my audition and then...

181
00:08:43,199 --> 00:08:45,433
-I met with Lorne, and everyone’s like...
-Right.

182
00:08:45,500 --> 00:08:48,033
"Oh, well, you met with Lorne,
that’s like, you’ve got the job."

183
00:08:48,133 --> 00:08:50,266
And I was like,
"Oh, my gosh, this is amazing."

184
00:08:50,666 --> 00:08:54,466
Like, I never heard anything and like
the summer and then the season started.

185
00:08:54,633 --> 00:08:56,633
And I was like, "Well I’m not there."

186
00:08:56,800 --> 00:08:59,066
-And what were you doing?
-I was living here.

187
00:08:59,133 --> 00:09:01,466
And then it was like, four shows in,
and they called me

188
00:09:01,533 --> 00:09:04,199
and they were like,
"Can you come next week?"

189
00:09:04,466 --> 00:09:07,199
I don’t think they told anyone
I was joining the cast.

190
00:09:07,333 --> 00:09:10,500
I came in and I was like, "Hi."
Just so awkward.

191
00:09:10,866 --> 00:09:12,366
I remember like when I first started,

192
00:09:12,433 --> 00:09:15,133
I was like, "Oh, the moment
I feel comfortable here,

193
00:09:15,400 --> 00:09:16,699
is when I have to leave."

194
00:09:16,900 --> 00:09:19,300
I remember year four, I was like,
"I’m done.

195
00:09:19,458 --> 00:09:22,495
I can’t do anything else with my voice."
Like I don’t, I really felt that way,

196
00:09:22,562 --> 00:09:24,363
"I have no more, I’ve done every accent,

197
00:09:24,699 --> 00:09:26,766
I don’t have any more characters in me,
like what?"

198
00:09:26,833 --> 00:09:27,666
That’s so funny.

199
00:09:27,766 --> 00:09:29,766
[jazz music playing]

200
00:09:31,300 --> 00:09:33,500
I don’t like being in front of people.

201
00:09:33,833 --> 00:09:37,400
Being in a character, I could get on stage
and talk for half an hour.

202
00:09:37,600 --> 00:09:39,199
-Yeah.
-Like, my biggest fear is like,

203
00:09:39,266 --> 00:09:43,000
being at a big dinner party and someone
be like, "Kristen, tell that story about,"

204
00:09:43,600 --> 00:09:45,833
and then I just immediately go into
the sweats.

205
00:09:45,900 --> 00:09:48,199
-Do you have any--
-I’m like, "Everyone’s looking at me.

206
00:09:48,333 --> 00:09:49,733
-What’s my mouth doing?"
-Yeah.

207
00:09:49,800 --> 00:09:51,466
Like that’s what I’m thinking in my head.

208
00:09:51,533 --> 00:09:54,600
It’s weird to describe myself as
like being funny. I don’t know why.

209
00:09:54,794 --> 00:09:58,397
-I know I have, it’s a weird... I...
-I think we can get past that.

210
00:09:59,300 --> 00:10:01,366
-I don’t know.
-When you go out to dinner

211
00:10:01,566 --> 00:10:04,400
with people that aren’t funny,
do you have trouble focusing?

212
00:10:04,533 --> 00:10:05,900
Someone will tell a story and then,

213
00:10:06,000 --> 00:10:08,533
"Yeah, and then we just you know,
we all left."

214
00:10:10,000 --> 00:10:13,100
And it’s silent, and like everyone’s fine,
but if that were me,

215
00:10:13,166 --> 00:10:14,533
-I would have a panic attack.
-Yes.

216
00:10:14,600 --> 00:10:17,766
And I always wonder, do they
know they just went in the toilet?

217
00:10:17,850 --> 00:10:19,719
-No.
-They do think everyone’s going to go,

218
00:10:19,785 --> 00:10:21,821
-"Oh, my God."
-"That happened?"

219
00:10:22,000 --> 00:10:25,699
-"Really? You just bumped into them."
-"And you knew them from before."

220
00:10:25,833 --> 00:10:26,900
[Jerry laughing]

221
00:10:27,333 --> 00:10:29,500
[jazz music playing]

222
00:10:31,866 --> 00:10:34,266
I get doughnuts sometimes for my birthday
instead of cake.

223
00:10:34,566 --> 00:10:35,533
That’s a great idea.

224
00:10:35,633 --> 00:10:37,900
And I had my birthday
when we were shooting Ghostbusters,

225
00:10:38,000 --> 00:10:40,433
and they got a huge doughnut, it was like,

226
00:10:40,833 --> 00:10:43,699
that big, with candles in it.
It was delicious.

227
00:10:43,909 --> 00:10:45,311
Really? I’m surprised.

228
00:10:45,378 --> 00:10:47,680
I know, but it was.
It was like a big doughnut.

229
00:10:47,800 --> 00:10:49,733
Like, how are they gonna
have the consistency?

230
00:10:49,815 --> 00:10:52,051
-This is not gonna be good. It was.
-Not gonna be good.

231
00:10:56,366 --> 00:10:57,666
You don’t have doughnuts, do you?

232
00:10:59,533 --> 00:11:02,333
No doughnuts. We have a lot of pie.

233
00:11:05,199 --> 00:11:07,433
-Peach pie is in season.
-[Jerry] What are you feeling?

234
00:11:07,666 --> 00:11:09,702
Peach pie in season. I just heard that.

235
00:11:09,800 --> 00:11:11,433
-Do you want the peach pie?
-Yes.

236
00:11:11,666 --> 00:11:12,666
Let's do that.

237
00:11:13,066 --> 00:11:15,133
[jazz music playing]

238
00:11:18,233 --> 00:11:20,333
We’re trying to figure out,
where’s the point?

239
00:11:20,533 --> 00:11:22,666
-[Kristen] Oh, I see, look. That’s it.
-[Jerry] Oh.

240
00:11:24,050 --> 00:11:26,052
-[Kristen] See the lift?
-[Jerry] I recognize it.

241
00:11:26,118 --> 00:11:28,954
-[Jerry] There’s the back border.
-Back border. As the chefs call it.

242
00:11:33,166 --> 00:11:37,100
How did you feel at that last SNL,
that fantastic thing they did for you?

243
00:11:37,466 --> 00:11:40,433
I waited months before I watched it.

244
00:11:40,600 --> 00:11:42,533
-Really?
-And I like lost it, oh, yeah.

245
00:11:42,733 --> 00:11:46,433
It was the hardest thing I had to do,
even though it was my decision to do it.

246
00:11:46,566 --> 00:11:48,966
Was there any discussion of you not going?

247
00:11:49,066 --> 00:11:52,166
No. It didn’t feel right to stay.

248
00:11:52,300 --> 00:11:54,333
I missed my family.
I needed to see daylight.

249
00:11:54,400 --> 00:11:56,166
-And like go to the dentist.
-It’s true.

250
00:11:56,233 --> 00:11:58,533
‘Cause you write all Tuesday night,
and then come home

251
00:11:58,600 --> 00:12:01,066
and you’d be in the car,
and people are running, up early.

252
00:12:01,133 --> 00:12:02,766
And I’m like, "What’s that like?"

253
00:12:02,900 --> 00:12:05,199
I do miss the quick puzzle pieces,
you know,

254
00:12:05,324 --> 00:12:08,260
like someone walks up to you, like,
"We have to cut 20 seconds out of that."

255
00:12:08,400 --> 00:12:10,100
-It’s fun, right?
-Yeah, it's so fun.

256
00:12:10,166 --> 00:12:12,333
-It’s like you’re a bomb squad.
-I know, you do.

257
00:12:12,500 --> 00:12:14,933
-Because your brain is working so fast...
-Yes.

258
00:12:15,000 --> 00:12:18,671
...to figure out a math problem
that has nothing to do with anything.

259
00:12:18,738 --> 00:12:20,573
-But then you figure it out.
-Yeah.

260
00:12:20,706 --> 00:12:21,974
And it’s one in the morning.

261
00:12:22,041 --> 00:12:24,510
Then it’s over and like,
"Oh, my God. How did we do that?"

262
00:12:24,577 --> 00:12:27,646
And it ended on time, and like,
"Okay, see you tomorrow."

263
00:12:31,450 --> 00:12:33,719
[Jerry] I don’t want to see anything
on anybody’s phone anymore.

264
00:12:33,933 --> 00:12:35,933
This thing has become...

265
00:12:36,066 --> 00:12:39,400
Just anyone’s hand reaching towards me
with a phone in it.

266
00:12:39,599 --> 00:12:42,833
I don’t care if you saw an alien changing
a tire on his saucer.

267
00:12:43,199 --> 00:12:44,766
I don’t care what you got.

268
00:12:44,933 --> 00:12:48,033
-I don’t care what it is.
-I don’t think they’ll have tires.

269
00:12:48,300 --> 00:12:49,800
That’s the whole point, they hover--

270
00:12:49,900 --> 00:12:51,900
-Well, that’s the joke.
-That is the joke.

271
00:12:51,966 --> 00:12:53,533
-The part I thought was funny.
-Yeah.

272
00:12:56,466 --> 00:12:59,166
Orthodontic care for children is
so down now.

273
00:12:59,211 --> 00:13:00,613
-When you were a kid...
-Yes--

274
00:13:00,679 --> 00:13:01,881
It was one chair, right?

275
00:13:02,014 --> 00:13:04,550
You go in there now, it’s like 20 chairs.

276
00:13:04,633 --> 00:13:06,199
They know exactly when to put them on.

277
00:13:06,333 --> 00:13:08,699
They start with the palate expander,
then the retainer.

278
00:13:08,833 --> 00:13:10,466
-Palate expander?
-Yeah.

279
00:13:10,599 --> 00:13:13,133
I wanted palate expanders
to be a Spider-Man villain.

280
00:13:13,599 --> 00:13:15,433
[both laughing]

281
00:13:16,166 --> 00:13:17,933
He goes like this, and people are like...

282
00:13:18,099 --> 00:13:19,800
[both laughing]

283
00:13:20,065 --> 00:13:22,334
[jazz music playing]

284
00:13:23,636 --> 00:13:26,071
[Jerry] There was a guy that used to have
a bullhorn on stage.

285
00:13:26,138 --> 00:13:27,373
His name was Glenn Super.

286
00:13:27,500 --> 00:13:28,800
He had the bullhorn,

287
00:13:28,900 --> 00:13:30,966
"This comes in handy a lot of times
in real life."

288
00:13:31,266 --> 00:13:34,133
And he would go,
"Some coffee would be nice."

289
00:13:34,233 --> 00:13:35,233
[chuckles]

290
00:13:35,500 --> 00:13:39,066
The whole setup of the thing,
the bullhorn, the restaurant,

291
00:13:39,133 --> 00:13:42,166
the person wants coffee,
that’s not the funny part.

292
00:13:42,366 --> 00:13:44,833
The funny part is he didn’t
even ask for it.

293
00:13:44,900 --> 00:13:45,800
Yes.

294
00:13:46,133 --> 00:13:47,500
-It’s just a comment.
-Yeah.

295
00:13:47,666 --> 00:13:49,533
If you would’ve said it another way,
or asked...

296
00:13:49,766 --> 00:13:52,433
-"May I have some more coffee?" That's...
-That's not funny.

297
00:13:52,633 --> 00:13:54,900
[jazz music playing]

298
00:13:56,933 --> 00:14:00,400
Did you see that billboard on La Cienega
for sugar models?

299
00:14:01,066 --> 00:14:02,666
-No.
-It said, "Sugar models,

300
00:14:03,000 --> 00:14:07,166
sugar dates, arrangements, social club."

301
00:14:07,400 --> 00:14:09,533
Maybe it’s a modeling agency,
maybe it’s a dessert.

302
00:14:09,633 --> 00:14:10,833
-Yeah.
-Maybe it's both.

303
00:14:10,933 --> 00:14:13,833
Maybe it’s models of things made of sugar?

304
00:14:13,919 --> 00:14:15,754
-It’s just a sweet shop is all.
-That's all.

305
00:14:15,821 --> 00:14:18,891
That’s all it is.
I want three bear claws a week.

306
00:14:18,958 --> 00:14:21,260
Mm-hmm, that’s my arrangement.
And sugar club.

307
00:14:21,327 --> 00:14:22,761
-Social club.
-Social club.

308
00:14:22,828 --> 00:14:24,964
People who just want to sit around
with dessert.

309
00:14:25,099 --> 00:14:26,966
-And talk to people.
-Yeah. About dessert.

310
00:14:27,166 --> 00:14:28,566
Yeah. That sounds like fun.

311
00:14:30,633 --> 00:14:33,333
-Should we call?
-Yeah, call them.

312
00:14:33,500 --> 00:14:36,000
I would like to see you answering
the phone at Sugar Models.

313
00:14:36,709 --> 00:14:39,245
-"Hello."
-[both laughing]

314
00:14:40,266 --> 00:14:43,266
"Sugar Models, connecting beautiful women
with affluent men."

315
00:14:45,900 --> 00:14:48,533
-Yeah. I don’t think this is...
-No, we don’t want to call that.

316
00:14:48,699 --> 00:14:50,866
Nope. We don’t want to do it.
Not from your phone.

317
00:14:52,300 --> 00:14:55,633
"Oh, Jerry Seinfeld’s calling." "Again?"

318
00:14:55,900 --> 00:14:57,800
-Then my wife sees it.
-"Jerry?

319
00:14:58,699 --> 00:15:00,233
Who is Sugar Models?"

320
00:15:00,366 --> 00:15:02,234
[both laughing]

321
00:15:03,633 --> 00:15:06,533
By the way, it was--
$20 dollars is the whole check.

322
00:15:07,133 --> 00:15:09,000
-That’s crazy.
-That’s crazy.

323
00:15:09,300 --> 00:15:13,333
Wait, two servings of eggs, a croissant,
and a piece of pie.

324
00:15:13,412 --> 00:15:16,382
-This place is frozen in time.
-Yeah, the eggs were three dollars.

325
00:15:16,448 --> 00:15:18,183
-Three dollars?
-Yes.

326
00:15:18,250 --> 00:15:19,618
What year is this?

327
00:15:19,718 --> 00:15:22,121
[upbeat music playing]

328
00:15:22,888 --> 00:15:23,856
[inaudible]

329
00:15:27,699 --> 00:15:28,733
[Kristen] Ooh, glasses.

330
00:15:30,033 --> 00:15:31,666
I feel like...

331
00:15:32,666 --> 00:15:34,966
I can make my own salsa.

332
00:15:37,000 --> 00:15:38,400
[Jerry] What do you think of these on me?

333
00:15:38,566 --> 00:15:41,300
You know, I think they actually
fit your head really well.

334
00:15:41,500 --> 00:15:44,233
Salespeople are always dishonest.
You’re being dishonest.

335
00:15:44,343 --> 00:15:46,211
-I’m not being dishonest.
-They don’t look good.

336
00:15:46,278 --> 00:15:47,546
I don’t look good.

337
00:15:47,613 --> 00:15:48,948
Would you suggest I buy these?

338
00:15:49,014 --> 00:15:51,350
I mean, yeah. You look great.

339
00:15:51,617 --> 00:15:54,787
-Horrible. Absolutely horrible.
-They make great gifts.

340
00:15:55,500 --> 00:15:56,599
Just kidding.

341
00:15:56,822 --> 00:15:58,424
[inaudible]

342
00:16:00,592 --> 00:16:02,528
[Jerry] Look at the Jeni’s Ice Cream.

343
00:16:02,594 --> 00:16:04,663
When they get the look of these
stores right...

344
00:16:04,733 --> 00:16:06,199
-Want to go in?
-It drives me nuts.

345
00:16:06,599 --> 00:16:08,400
-At Jeni’s?
-Yeah.

346
00:16:09,168 --> 00:16:11,103
[Jerry laughs]

347
00:16:11,370 --> 00:16:14,273
-[horn honks]
-Excuse me, we have to get to Jeni's.

348
00:16:14,433 --> 00:16:15,566
[both laughing]

349
00:16:16,008 --> 00:16:17,676
[upbeat music playing]

350
00:16:29,733 --> 00:16:31,866
-[Kristen] I’ll do a little heart.
-[woman] Thank you.

351
00:16:31,924 --> 00:16:33,726
Thanks. I wrote "I hate you."

352
00:16:35,260 --> 00:16:38,163
[upbeat music continues]

353
00:16:41,533 --> 00:16:42,866
[Jerry] Know your way home from here?

354
00:16:43,833 --> 00:16:44,933
To my actual house?

355
00:16:45,666 --> 00:16:47,466
Is there another home that
you would live in?

356
00:16:49,133 --> 00:16:51,233
That’s your house,
and you go back to your life now.

357
00:16:51,300 --> 00:16:52,300
Oh. All right.

358
00:16:52,733 --> 00:16:55,666
[Jerry] By the way, House of Pies
sounds dirtier than Sugar Models.

359
00:16:55,781 --> 00:16:57,416
[Kristen laughs]

360
00:17:06,658 --> 00:17:08,660
I’m gonna take one more picture of it.

361
00:17:12,865 --> 00:17:15,000
Oh, it’s so cool. [gasps]

362
00:17:15,367 --> 00:17:21,673
My dogs are here. Hi, guys.
I haven’t seen you in so long. Hello.

363
00:17:21,740 --> 00:17:23,976
[Jerry] This is a nice dog. Oh, my gosh.

364
00:17:24,043 --> 00:17:25,411
-[Kristen] Step-dog.
-[Jerry] Yeah.

365
00:17:25,477 --> 00:17:26,979
[both laughing]

